+- **NSFW consent grant/revoke route (`POST /api/v1/nsfw/consent`)** — the consent ledger (`nsfw_consent_state`) and the gate that reads it existed, but nothing in production ever granted consent, so every NSFW write denied with `consent_required`. The new route (`src/routes/nsfw/consent.ts`, mounted from `src/routes/nsfw/index.ts`) writes a `given` or `revoked` row for (session user, chat) through the existing transactional `recordNsfwConsent`. The body DTO `NsfwConsentBody` (`src/validation/schemas/nsfw.ts`) is closed (`additionalProperties: false`) and reuses the ledger's own `given`/`revoked` vocabulary; the user id comes from the session, never the body, and the raw request object is re-checked against the closed DTO so a client-supplied `userId` is a 422 rather than a silently dropped key. The caller must participate in the target chat (participant row, or the chat's creator) — otherwise the route would be a consent-row writer for arbitrary chats. The same change makes `chatId` REQUIRED on the chat-scoped write DTOs (`NsfwIntimacyActionBody`, `NsfwSeductionAttemptBody`, `NsfwEncounterCreateBody`): omitting the chat id used to skip the consent stage entirely, so a chatless NSFW write is now a 422 at the boundary.
 - **Harness/async job state surfaces as chat messages (TASK-harness-inflight-job-handles, first slice)** — every auto-generation job now leaves an append-only `job_status` trail in the chat timeline: `reply.ts` posts the initial `pending` row keyed by the tracked `x-request-id` before `triggerAutoGeneration` is kicked off, and the pipeline (`auto-generation.ts` via the new `job-status-bridge.ts`) journals each progress step (`preparing`/`generating`/`storing`) plus a terminal `complete`/`failed` row that carries the harness `turnId` (attempt id) in `metadata`, so `.harness/executions.jsonl` lines join to chat rows via `metadata.turnId`. Rows are `content_type='job_status'`, `role='system'`, `metadata` `{jobId, requestId, op, state[, step][, turnId]}` — one NEW row per distinct (job, state, step) event, never update-in-place: message rows carry `record_hash` and the journal treats them as immutable events, with a per-process memo collapsing double progress callbacks to exactly-once rows. The writer (`src/chat/service/job-status.ts`) is best-effort like `injectPartyNarration` — failures are structured-logged, never thrown into the request or generation path. Delivery rides the existing per-chat SSE: the bridge pushes a `job-status` event into the chat's live stream buffer when one exists (never creates one), the frontend renders it as a one-line ⚙ status under the stream (`chat-generations.ts`), and persisted rows get a compact text-only one-line renderer in `message-list.html`'s system branch. Display-only by design: appending rows never schedules generation, so the pause/cascade gates stay authoritative and the notification-loop class is out of scope. No migration — `job_status` is a text-column enum value on `MessageContentType`.
 
